.TH FLATTEN 1 "2026-08-07" "flatten.sh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
flatten.sh \- move files up N directory levels out of deep nesting
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flatten.sh
[\fILEVELS\fR]
[\fIDIRECTORY\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B flatten.sh
pulls all files up by the given number of directory levels, undoing deep
nesting such as
.IR dir/sub/sub/file
becoming
.IR dir/file .
It operates on files only; directories themselves are not moved.
.P
The work is done in
.I LEVELS
passes. In each pass every file at depth two or deeper is moved up exactly
one level (from its parent directory into its grandparent), and then any
directories left empty are deleted. Files therefore climb one level per
pass, so a
.I LEVELS
of 3 moves a file up three levels.
.P
When a move would collide with an existing file at the destination, the
moved file is renamed by appending an underscore and a counter to its base
name (\fB_\fR\fI1\fR, \fB_\fR\fI2\fR, ...) before the extension, e.g.
.I photo.png
becomes
.IR photo_1.png .
.P
The first positional argument is the number of levels and defaults to
.BR 1 .
The second positional argument is the target directory and defaults to the
current directory
.BR . .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I LEVELS
Number of levels to pull files up. Must be a positive integer. Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.I DIRECTORY
Root directory to flatten. Must be an existing directory. Default:
.BR . .
.SH OPTIONS
None. There are no command-line flags; both parameters are positional.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
\fITARGET\fR is not a directory, or
\fILEVELS\fR is missing, non-numeric, or less than 1. An error message is
printed before exiting.
.SH EXAMPLES
Flatten one level in the current directory:
.IP
.EX
flatten.sh
.EE
.P
Pull files up three levels:
.IP
.EX
flatten.sh 3
.EE
.P
Flatten a specific directory two levels:
.IP
.EX
flatten.sh 2 ~/Downloads/nested
.EE
.P
Conflict handling \-\- both
.I parent/a/photo.png
and
.I parent/photo.png
exist: the deeper file is moved as
.IR photo_1.png .
.SH NOTES
Empty directories below the target are removed by the script as files are
moved out. A
.B 0
or negative level count is rejected even though a single 0-value pass would
otherwise be a no-op.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mv (1),
.BR find (1)
